{
  "dom_id": "step-q315980",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14095273/assets/e13f4de3d3d66ba1205b3da8cbe8abec300a94d2e53453af3c9c12667e1d7907.png)\n\nA spherical soap bubble is expanding, causing its enclosed volume to increase at a rate of $24\\pi \\text{\u00a0}{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$ How fast is the radius increasing at the moment when the radius is $3\\text{\u00a0}\\text{cm}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe radius of the bubble is increasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $r$ be the radius of the bubble and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=24\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=3\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the volume of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=24\\pi $ and $r=3,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi (3)^{2} \\\\ 24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 36\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when the radius is of the bubble is $3\\,\\text{cm},$ the radius is increasing at a rate of  $\\frac{2}{3}$ $\\text{\u00a0}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$",
